.PP
\f[CR]\-\-cumulative\f[R] is omitted to save space, it works like
\f[CR]\-H\f[R] but with a zero starting balance.
.SH Lot reporting
Automated lot tracking and capital gains reporting is one of the main
new features of hledger 2.
Your testing and feedback are important for making it better.
.PP
When you buy (acquire) some amount of an investment commodity (a lot),
it can be important (depending on your local tax rules) to keep track of
its original cost and acquisition date (cost basis), so that when you
sell (dispose) it, you can calculate capital gains (or losses), and
document how you satisfied the rules about disposal order, short term vs
long term gains, and so on.
.PP
For many kinds of investment, each individual lot has its own cost
basis, which must be tracked even if the lot is reduced, split up, or
transferred.
Also lots may need to be moved and disposed of in a prescribed order.
All this can be very hard to keep track of by hand, so usually it is
done by an investment broker, cryptocurrency exchange, or specialised
tax software.
Now, you can also do it yourself with hledger.
.PP
hledger\(aqs lot tracking builds on the design first shipped in Ledger,
then improved in Beancount.
hledger understands several kinds of notation describing lots.
You can record all details explicitly; or use more convenient
low\-boilerplate entries, and it will infer the missing parts.
hledger checks lot entries, tracks and infers lot movements (reporting
any problems, such as disposal of nonexistent lots), and calculates
capital gains when lots are sold.
.PP
For a more technical version of what\(aqs in this manual, see
SPEC\-lots.
.SS How to enable or disable lot tracking
Lot tracking can be enabled in two ways:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Per posting:\f[R] write explicit curly\-brace lot annotations like
in Ledger or Beancount, or write explicit lot subaccount names.
Only these postings will be tracked lotfully.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Per commodity:\f[R] declare the commodity lotful with a
\f[CR]lots\f[R] tag.
All of its postings are tracked lotfully, and the annotations are
inferred for you.
This is convenient, and recommended.
.PP
A posting with any of these is called a lot posting.
If you want a commodity tracked lotfully in only some accounts, use
annotations rather than the \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag.
Or if it should be tracked everywhere except certain accounts (eg
tax\-sheltered accounts where cost basis doesn\(aqt matter), use the
\f[CR]lots\f[R] tag, and add a \f[CR]lots: NONE\f[R] tag to those
accounts\(aq declarations to disable lot tracking there.
.PP
The disposal order (AKA cost basis method \- which lots are consumed
first) is FIFO by default; or as set by a \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag
\f[I]value\f[R] on the commodity or account declaration (account wins),
eg \f[CR]lots: LIFO\f[R]; or chosen explicitly per disposal with a lot
selector.
See Cost basis methods.
.PP
Sometimes you may want to disable lots/gains processing, to silence
lot\-related errors when you are working with incomplete journals (eg,
when piping hledger print into another hledger command).
For this, use the \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-lots\f[R] flag, or just
\f[CR]\-I\f[R].
.SS First lots example
hledger\(aqs lot tracking does not require much extra notation.
Here is a small example, using \(at syntax.
(Lot reporting example below shows other styles.):
.IP
.EX
commodity AAPL  ; lots:

2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:cash     \-$500
    assets:stocks      10 AAPL \(at $50

2026\-02\-01 sell some
    assets:stocks      \-5 AAPL \(at $70
    assets:cash      $350
.EE
.PP
Lot tracking is activated for AAPL by the \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag, so
hledger will
.IP \(bu 2
keep track of each lot that\(aqs acquired (and optionally show them in
reports)
.IP \(bu 2
dispose of lots in the right order (FIFO by default)
.IP \(bu 2
calculate the resulting capital gain, and add the gain postings if
missing
.IP \(bu 2
check for many kinds of error (such as selling more than you have).
.PP
\f[CR]print\f[R] shows the inferred gain postings:
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:cash                                $\-500
    assets:stocks                                 10 AAPL \(at $50

2026\-02\-01 sell some
    assets:stocks                                 \-5 AAPL \(at $70
    assets:cash                                 $350
    revenues:gain                              $\-100
    equity:unrealised\-gain                      $100
.EE
.PP
Adding \f[CR]\-\-lots\f[R] shows the lots, as subaccounts.
(This flag works with all reports.):
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print \-\-lots
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:cash                                $\-500
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}               10 AAPL \(at $50

2026\-02\-01 sell some
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}               \-5 AAPL \(at $70
    assets:cash                                 $350
    revenues:gain                              $\-100
    equity:unrealised\-gain                      $100
.EE
.PP
Or use \f[CR]print \-a\f[R] (short for \f[CR]\-\-all\f[R]) to show
maximum detail:
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print \-a
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:cash                                $\-500
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}               10 AAPL {$50} \(at $50  ; ptype: acquire

2026\-02\-01 sell some
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}               \-5 AAPL {2026\-01\-15, $50} \(at $70  ; ptype: dispose
    assets:cash                                 $350
    revenues:gain                              $\-100  ; ptype: rgain, generated\-posting:
    equity:unrealised\-gain                      $100  ; ptype: ugain, generated\-posting:
.EE
.PP
Note, hledger is quite good at analysing lot entries but it won\(aqt
understand every possible shape; so if anything looks wrong, check the
\f[CR]print \-a\f[R] output, and if necessary rewrite the entry in a
different form.
.PP
Once you have lot entries, \f[CR]holdings\f[R] will show an overview of
your investments \- units held, per\-unit and total cost and value,
realised and unrealised gain, and XIRR (extended internal rate of
return).
To see all of these, add at least one market price declaration, eg:
.IP
.EX
P 2026\-03\-31 AAPL $72
.EE
.PP
Then:
.IP
.EX
$ hledger holdings \-e 2026\-04\-01
Holdings on 2026\-03\-31

               ||       Date  Age   Units  Avg cost  Price  Cost  Value  Weight  UGain  UGain%  RGain     XIRR
===============++==============================================================================================
 assets:stocks || 2026\-01\-15  75d  5 AAPL       $50    $72  $250   $360  100.0%   $110   44.0%   $100  1865.3% 
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-++\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
               ||                                           $250   $360  100.0%   $110   44.0%   $100  1865.3% 
.EE
.SS Lot concepts
.SS Cost basis annotations
Each acquisition of an investment creates a \f[I]lot\f[R], with a
\f[I]cost basis\f[R].
In hledger, a lot\(aqs cost basis has 2\-3 parts:
.IP "1." 3
The nominal acquisition date (required).
Usually this is the date you acquired the lot.
.IP "2." 3
A short text label (optional).
This can be used to distinguish lots acquired on the same date.
.IP "3." 3
The nominal acquisition cost (required).
Usually this is what you paid for it.
.PP
In the journal, we can write \f[I]cost basis annotations\f[R], enclosed
in {} after an amount.
The syntax is described in Cost basis.
These cost basis annotations often mention only part of the cost basis,
typically the cost.
Here are some examples:
.IP
.EX
{$50}
{1.000.000,33 EUR}
{2026\-01\-15, $50}
{2026\-01\-15, \(dq12:05\(dq, $50}
{}
.EE
.SS Lotful commodities
A more convenient way to record lot transactions, is to declare
commodities as \f[I]lotful\f[R], by adding a \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag in
their declaration.
Eg:
.IP
.EX
commodity AAPL          ; lots:
.EE
.PP
This tells hledger that postings involving these commodities always
involve lots, so it will infer cost basis annotations automatically, and
you won\(aqt need to write them in the journal.
.PP
(The \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag can optionally have a value, specifying the
order for disposing lots, discussed later in Cost basis methods.
Note, account declarations can also have a \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag, but
there it does not declare lotfulness, only disposal order; or, with the
special value \f[CR]NONE\f[R], it disables lot tracking in that account
and its subaccounts.
Eg for a tax\-sheltered account where cost basis is irrelevant:
.IP
.EX
account assets:ira      ; lots: NONE
.EE
.PP
Postings there are not lot\-tracked and get no lot subaccounts or gain
postings \- unless they have explicit lot annotations, which always
enable tracking.
Moving a lotful commodity from a tracked account into such an account is
a disposal; moving it out again needs a cost basis or price on the
receiving posting.)
.SS Lot subaccounts
Internally, hledger tracks each lot in a subaccount, named like the cost
basis.
You don\(aqt need to write these subaccounts in the journal; hledger
infers them automatically.
They are hidden from reports by default, since there can be many lots.
To show them, just add the \f[CR]\-\-lots\f[R] flag to any report (the
holdings command is designed for viewing them).
Eg:
.IP
.EX
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:stocks    10 AAPL {$50}
    assets:cash     \-$500
.EE
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:stocks    10 AAPL {$50}
    assets:cash              $\-500

$ hledger print \-\-lots
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}    10 AAPL {$50}
    assets:cash                                $\-500
.EE
.PP
If you do write a lot subaccount in the journal, it is equivalent to
writing a cost basis annotation on the amount:
.IP
.EX
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}    10 AAPL
    assets:cash
.EE
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print 
2026\-01\-15 buy
    assets:stocks    10 AAPL {2026\-01\-15, $50}
    assets:cash
.EE
.PP
Unlike cost basis annotations, lot subaccount names must be complete,
including all cost basis parts \- date, label if any, and cost.
.PP
Note this enclosing the leaf account name (final account name component)
in \f[CR]{\f[R] and \f[CR]}\f[R] is reserved syntax for lot subaccounts,
and hledger will report an error if what\(aqs inside the braces is is
not a valid lot name.
This is a breaking change from hledger 1.x, which had no special
handling for such names.
If you have such an account name and don\(aqt want hledger to reject it,
you can pass \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-lots\f[R] (or \f[CR]\-I\f[R]) to skip lot
processing entirely, causing \f[CR]:{...}\f[R] to be treated as ordinary
subaccounts.
.PP
When strictly checking account names, lot subaccounts are ignored \- you
only need to declare the base account (eg \f[CR]assets:stocks\f[R]), not
the lot subaccounts.
.SS Lot ids
A lot id is a unique identifier for that lot, used internally by
hledger.
It is the cost basis date, followed by the label if any, with a space
between them.
.PP
Lot ids also record the order in which lots were acquired.
So if multiple lots of a commodity are acquired on the same date, they
must have distinct, sortable labels.
Eg the time of the event, in \f[CR]HH:MM\f[R] format, could be used.
Or if no labels are provided, hledger will add sequentially numbered
labels starting from \f[CR]0001\f[R].
.PP
Acquisitions with these cost basis annotations would produce those lot
ids:
.IP
.EX
{2026\-01\-01, $50}           ; → \(dq2026\-01\-01\(dq
{2026\-01\-02, \(dq12:30\(dq, $50}  ; → \(dq2026\-01\-02 12:30\(dq
{2026\-01\-03, $50}           ; → \(dq2026\-01\-03 0001\(dq
{2026\-01\-03, $50}           ; → \(dq2026\-01\-04 0002\(dq
.EE
.SS Cost basis vs transacted cost
In acquisition transactions, hledger allows the cost basis
(\f[CR]{}\f[R], call it \f[CR]B\f[R]) and transacted cost
(\f[CR]\(at\f[R], call it \f[CR]T\f[R]) to be different, for
compatibility with other plain text accounting apps.
But the preferred style is to keep these the same; typically we write
just one of them and let the other be inferred.
.PP
You can check that \f[CR]B\f[R] == \f[CR]T\f[R] everywhere by running
\f[CR]hledger check basis\f[R].
This catches {}/\(at mismatches which would silently miscalculate
capital gains.
Note, this check uses strict equality, not
equality\-within\-local\-precisions like transaction balancing or
recorded gain checking, (because an inexact basis annotation would
persist and be amplified at disposal time).
So if you write an explicit cost basis annotation, use sufficient
precision.
If the amount is a non\-terminating decimal, you won\(aqt be able to
record it explicitly \- instead write \f[CR]{}\f[R] or
\f[CR]TOTALCOST\f[R] and let hledger infer it.
.PP
Some real\-world situations can produce a cost basis that differs from
what was paid \- gifts (carryover basis), inheritance (stepped\-up
basis), stock options (NSOs, ISOs, ESPPs, RSUs), wash sales, corporate
actions, and so on.
For more background, see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cost_basis.
.PP
However, in these cases the preferred style can still be used: record
\f[CR]B = T\f[R] on the asset posting, and fund any difference via a
separate income, equity, or asset posting \- rather than expressing the
difference as \f[CR]{B} \(at T\f[R] with \f[CR]B ≠ T\f[R] on the asset
itself.
The Acquire section below shows an example.
.SS Cost basis precision
An inferred cost basis can be a non\-terminating decimal (eg
\f[CR]3 ABC \(at\(at $10\f[R] gives a $10/3 unit cost).
Internally hledger keeps such costs at high precision, and calculates
gains from the unrounded value; but in lot names it displays at most 8
decimal digits (eg \f[CR]{2021\-03\-08, $3.33333333}\f[R]), or more if
the commodity\(aqs declared display style has more (eg
\f[CR]commodity $0.0000000000\f[R]).
A lot\(aqs displayed name always works as a selector, even when it is a
rounded display of a longer inferred cost.
.PP
Two precision\-related cautions:
.IP \(bu 2
When lots migrate to a new journal file as text (eg with
\f[CR]close \-\-lots\f[R]), only the displayed digits survive: the new
file\(aqs cost basis is exactly the displayed value, so subsequent gains
can differ from the old file\(aqs by a tiny amount (up to half of the
last displayed digit, per unit, once).
If you track commodities needing finer cost precision (eg micro\-priced
tokens, or costs denominated in a cryptocurrency), declare a wider
display style for the cost commodity up front.
.IP \(bu 2
Changing a commodity\(aqs declared display precision can change how
inferred costs render in lot names.
Any lot names or selectors recorded in the journal with the old
rendering will then stop matching: hledger reports \(dqno lots matching
\&...\(dq, listing the account\(aqs actual lots.
Recovery is simple but manual: update the recorded names/selectors to
the new rendering, which is shown in the error message (or by
\f[CR]print \-\-lots\f[R]).
Selectors using just the date (and label) don\(aqt embed a cost and are
unaffected \- one more reason to prefer them.
.SS Lot movements
hledger understands three kinds of event involving lots.
Other real\-world lot events can usually be modelled using combinations
of these.
.SS Acquire
A positive lot posting in an asset account creates a new lot.
.IP
.EX
2026\-01\-01 buy shares
    assets:cash     \-$500
    assets:broker      10 ETSY {$50}
.EE
.PP
The cost basis can be specified explicitly with \f[CR]{}\f[R] on the
amount, inferred from the lot subaccount name, or inferred from the
transacted cost.
For lotful commodities, even a bare positive posting (no \f[CR]{}\f[R]
or \f[CR]\(at\f[R]) can be detected as an acquire, with cost inferred
from the transaction\(aqs other postings.
.PP
Acquire postings may carry a per\-unit (\f[CR]{}\f[R]) or total
(\f[CR]{{}}\f[R]) cost basis annotation, and a per\-unit
(\f[CR]\(at\f[R]) or total (\f[CR]\(at\(at\f[R]) transacted cost.
See Cost basis vs transacted cost for the recommended style.
.PP
Here is a worked example: an acquisition where the cost basis differs
from the commodity\(aqs current market price.
With GOLD currently priced at $5000, you receive a gift carrying its
original cost basis of $3000/oz:
.IP
.EX
P 2026\-05\-01 GOLD $5000

2026\-05\-01 gift from Aunt, with carryover basis of $3000
    income:gifts         $\-6000
    assets:stock              2 GOLD {$3000}
.EE
.PP
The transacted cost is $3000 (same as the basis), not $5000.
And the recorded gift income is $6000 (the gift\(aqs current market
value is $10000, but remember when you dispose you\(aqll have to declare
$2000/oz more capital gain, $4000 more in total).
.SS Transfer
A matching pair of negative/positive lotful postings moves one or more
existing lots between accounts, preserving their cost basis.
.IP
.EX
2026\-05\-01 transfer to another broker
    assets:broker     \-10 ETSY
    assets:broker2     10 ETSY
.EE
.PP
Transfer postings should not have a transacted price, and the total
quantities sent and received must match: transferred lots keep their
identity.
Within that, source and destination postings need not pair up one to one
\- one source posting can feed several destination accounts, or several
sources one destination.
The destination amount, or the source amount, can also be elided, even
when the other posting carries a lot selector (eg
\f[CR]assets:broker \-10 ETSY {2026\-01\-01}\f[R] balanced by a bare
\f[CR]assets:broker2\f[R] posting, or vice versa).
A consequence: a disposal entry mistakenly written without a selling
price, and with the other amount left implicit, looks like a lot
transfer, and is quietly read as one, without raising an error.
If in doubt, \f[CR]print \-a\f[R] shows how an entry was read.
If the destination receives less than the source sends (eg due to a fee
deducted by an exchange), record the fee as its own posting in the same
commodity (eg \f[CR]expenses:fees 0.001 ETH \(at $3000\f[R], or without
the price); several fee postings which together add up to the missing
quantity also work.
hledger then automatically splits the source posting into a transfer
portion and disposal portion(s), so that the fee disposals are detected
correctly.
Otherwise, mismatched sent/received totals are an error.
If a fee posting has a transacted price, its disposal portion carries it
and a gain is calculated; otherwise the disposal is priceless and no
gain is calculated.
The fee\(aqs disposal selects lots before the transfer does, using the
cost basis method in effect \- so under the default FIFO method, the fee
consumes the oldest lots and the transfer carries the rest.
\f[CR]print \-\-lots\f[R] (or \f[CR]print \-a\f[R]) shows the split form
explicitly, so the output round\-trips correctly.
.PP
Conversely, if the destination receives \f[I]more\f[R] than the source
sends (eg a reclaimed fee, or dust from a past bookkeeping error), a fee
can\(aqt explain that, and the extra should be recorded as its own
acquisition: give it a real or dummy cost basis (or price) annotation,
which keeps it out of the transfer matching.
Eg:
.IP
.EX
2026\-05\-01 transfer, plus a reclaimed fee
    assets:broker      \-10 ETH
    assets:broker2      10 ETH
    expenses:fees   \-0.001 ETH
    assets:broker2   0.001 ETH {$0}
.EE
.PP
hledger does not automatically capitalise fees into cost basis (as some
tax treatments allow, for purchase or transfer fees).
To capitalise an acquisition fee, fold it into the acquisition cost:
.IP
.EX
2026\-01\-15 buy 10 AAPL at $50, plus a $10 commission, capitalised
    assets:stocks     10 AAPL \(at\(at $510
    assets:cash      \-$510
.EE
.PP
This lot\(aqs cost basis is $51 per share.
(Recording the commission as a separate expense posting would instead
keep it out of the basis.)
Capitalising an in\-kind transfer fee \- keeping the remaining units\(aq
total basis unchanged \- requires disposing of the position at its basis
price (producing no gain) and re\-acquiring the remainder with the
combined basis, eg:
.IP
.EX
; earlier: bought 5 ABC for $20 ($4 each); now 0.1 ABC is deducted in transit:
2020\-04\-05 transfer with 0.1 ABC fee, capitalised
    assets:broker1     \-5 ABC {} \(at $4
    assets:broker2      4.9 ABC {$4.08163265} \(at\(at $20
.EE
.PP
Note this re\-acquisition starts a new lot, with a new date unless you
write the original date in its basis annotation; usually fees are simply
expensed instead.
.SS Dispose
A negative lot posting sells from one or more existing lots.
.IP
.EX
2026\-08\-01 sell at a gain
    assets:broker2     \-10 ETSY {$50} \(at $90   ; selecting the lot by specific identification
    assets:cash       $900
    revenues:gain    $\-400                    ; gain can be recorded explicitly or left implicit
.EE
.PP
The disposal posting must have a transacted price (the selling price),
either explicit or inferred: $90 here.
(Exception: a priceless disposal is allowed when it is an in\-kind
outflow \- when the entry\(aqs non\-asset postings receive the disposed
units: the same commodity, in the same total quantity.
Eg a transfer fee deducted in the commodity, or an in\-kind donation,
possibly split across postings.
This is a simple way to record such outflows when you don\(aqt need a
gain calculated: the disposed units leave their lot(s) carrying their
own cost basis, so the remaining units\(aq basis is unchanged, but no
gain or loss is recognised and the receiving posting holds commodity
units, not priced.
To have the gain calculated, record the receiving posting with a
transacted price.)
.PP
When the gain postings are inferred (not written explicitly), hledger
sizes them from the disposal side only: the sum of
\f[CR]quantity × (B − T)\f[R] over the entry\(aqs dispose postings.
Acquire postings in the same entry don\(aqt contribute to the inferred
gain (any acquire\-side \f[CR]B ≠ T\f[R] would be rejected at load time
anyway, see Acquire above).
.SS Cost basis methods
If a lot transfer or a lot disposal doesn\(aqt specifically identify the
lot(s) involved, hledger selects from the available lots automatically,
using a \f[I]cost basis method\f[R] (AKA disposal method / reduction
method / booking method).
.PP
The default method is FIFO (first in, first out).
You can override this with a \f[CR]lots\f[R] tag value on the commodity
or account declaration.
(An account tag will take precedence; on an account, the tag requires a
method value.)
Eg:
.IP
.EX
commodity FUND                  ; lots: AVERAGE
account assets:stocks           ; lots: LIFO
.EE
.PP
These methods are supported:
.PP
