.PP
\f[B]SPECID\f[R] (specific identification) is what you\(aqre using when
the journal entry contains explicit lot selectors like
\f[CR]{2026\-01\-15, $50}\f[R] or \f[CR]{$50}\f[R], or an explicit lot
subaccount like \f[CR]assets:broker:{2026\-01\-15, $50}\f[R].
Selecting by date (and label, if any) is recommended.
Cost\-only selectors like \f[CR]{$50}\f[R] are less robust: since costs
displayed in lot names may be rounded (see Cost basis precision),
distinct lots can display the same cost, and a cost\-only selector could
then match more lots than intended.
(With SPECID this is reported as an ambiguity error; with other methods
the lots are consumed in the method\(aqs order.)
.PP
\f[B]FIFO\f[R] (first in first out).
Dispose of the oldest lot first.
.PP
\f[B]LIFO\f[R] (last in first out).
Dispose of the youngest lot first.
.PP
\f[B]HIFO\f[R] (highest\-in\-first\-out) selects the lot with the
highest per\-unit cost first, which can be useful for tax optimization.
.PP
\f[B]AVERAGE\f[R] maintains an average per\-unit cost shared by every
lot in the per\-account pool.
Each new acquisition recalculates this average cost and applies it to
every lot in the pool.
Disposals use this average cost, and consume lots in FIFO order (so
acquisition dates remain meaningful for calculating
short\-term/long\-term holding\-period).
Under AVERAGE, the lot subaccount name omits the cost component
(\f[CR]{2026\-01\-15}\f[R] rather than \f[CR]{2026\-01\-15, $50}\f[R])
so it stays stable across acquisitions.
The average cost can be seen in the holdings report\(aqs Avg cost
column, which shows each pool\(aqs running average as of the report
date.
It also appears in disposal postings\(aq cost basis annotations and
inferred gain amounts, shown by \f[CR]print \-x\f[R].
(\f[CR]print \-x\f[R] shows acquire postings with their acquisition cost
basis, not the average.)
Transferring lots into an average\-cost account recalculates the
pool\(aqs average, just like an acquisition at the transferred lots\(aq
cost; transferring lots out carries the pool\(aqs average cost with
them.
Note averaging loses information: a lot\(aqs original cost cannot be
recovered by transferring it back out of the pool.
.PP
All of these methods select lots from the account mentioned in the
posting.
But the \f[B]*ALL\f[R] variants (FIFOALL, LIFOALL, HIFOALL) additionally
validate that these lots are the ones that would be chosen if
considering the global pool (all accounts holding that commodity).
So if there is a more appropriate lot in another account (eg an older
lot when using FIFOALL), they will raise an error showing which account
holds it.
This is useful if you need to enforce a global disposal order across all
accounts (brokers, exchanges, wallets etc).
Since these methods involve all accounts, a commodity using one must use
it in every account that holds the commodity; hledger checks this, so
it\(aqs best to declare a *ALL method on the commodity rather than on
accounts.
.PP
\f[B]AVERAGEALL\f[R] is to AVERAGE what FIFOALL is to FIFO: the pool
spans all accounts holding the commodity, so the running cost is a
single global value and an acquisition in one account updates the cost
basis on lots in every other account too.
.SS Changing the cost basis method
hledger recalculates all lots on each run, using the currently declared
methods.
So changing a declared method also reinterprets past history under the
new method.
Sometimes that\(aqs fine; but usually you\(aqll want past disposals to
keep their original lot selections and gains (tax authorities generally
expect a method change to apply only going forward).
Things to know:
.IP \(bu 2
Disposals recorded without explicit lot selectors or gain amounts will
select different lots under the new method, changing their cost bases
and realised gains.
.IP \(bu 2
Disposals which do record the old method\(aqs selections \- as explicit
gain amounts, lot selectors, lot subaccount names, or balance assertions
\- will be checked against the new method\(aqs selections, and any
differences will be reported as errors.
.PP
hledger doesn\(aqt yet support declaring different methods for different
time periods, but you can make a method change apply only to the future,
in either of two ways:
.IP "1." 3
Make past disposals fully explicit, with lot selectors and gain amounts,
so they no longer depend on the declared method (\f[CR]print \-x\f[R]
and \f[CR]print \-\-lots\f[R] can help with this).
Then change the method.
.IP "2." 3
Or, start a new journal file at the changeover date, using
\f[CR]close \-\-clopen \-\-lots\f[R] to carry the current lots into it,
and declare the new method in the new file.
.SS Gain postings
Each disposal transaction will have a balanced pair of postings
recording the capital gain (or loss):
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]realised gain\f[R] (rgain) on a Gain\-type account \- the
gain/loss, negated
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]unrealised gain\f[R] (ugain) on an UnrealisedGain\-type account \-
same amount, opposite sign
.PP
Conceptually, the market value of a lot fluctuates from acquisition
onward, accumulating unrealised gain or loss.
At disposal, that accumulated unrealised gain is reclassified as
realised gain.
.PP
Gain postings can be left implicit (and hledger will infer them); or
they can be written explicitly in the journal.
Recording gains below shows examples of both.
.PP
In inferred gain postings, the gain amount will be rounded to the
entry\(aqs local precision for the gain commodity (or if the local
precision is zero, two decimal digits will be shown \- except when both
of those digits are zero).
More decimals can be seen by increasing the display precision (eg
\f[CR]hledger print \-\-round=soft \-c \(aq$1.0000\(aq\f[R]).
.PP
In explicit gain postings, the amount must also be written.
(Usually hledger fills in missing amounts, but not in gain postings.)
.SS Gain and UnrealisedGain accounts
The \f[B]\f[CB]Gain\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]\f[CB]G\f[B]\f[R]) and
\f[B]\f[CB]UnrealisedGain\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]\f[CB]U\f[B]\f[R]) account
types are subtypes of Revenue and Equity respectively.
Declaring an account with the G type (and optionally one with the U
type) serves three purposes:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Recognising gain postings\f[R] \- explicit gain postings which use
the G account will be detected reliably by account type, and error
messages may be clearer.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Customising account names\f[R] \- inferred gain postings will use
the first\-declared G and U accounts, rather than the defaults
(\f[CR]revenues:gain\f[R] and \f[CR]equity:unrealised\-gain\f[R]).
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Categorising\f[R] \- when reporting, you can match on the G (or U)
account type specifically.
.PP
G and U are inferred from conventional English account names (eg
\f[CR]revenues:gain\f[R], \f[CR]income:capital\-gains\f[R],
\f[CR]equity:unrealised\-gain\f[R], \f[CR]equity:unrealized gains\f[R];
see the regex table under Account types).
You can also declare them explicitly:
.IP
.EX
account revenues:capital gain   ; type: G
account equity:unrealised gain  ; type: U
.EE
.PP
Why do we post both gains and losses to a revenue account ?
It\(aqs more convenient than using separate revenue and expense
accounts, and the sign keeps things correct.
.SS Recording gains
In the journal, you can write disposal transactions in four main styles,
with different tradeoffs between brevity, error checking, and
robustness.
From least to most explicit, they are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Style 1:\f[R] Don\(aqt write gain postings (let hledger infer them)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Style 2:\f[R] Write the rgain posting, using a non\-G account
(undeclared gain posting)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Style 3:\f[R] Write the rgain posting, using a G account (declared
gain posting, more reliable)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Style 4:\f[R] Write both rgain and ugain postings, using G/U
accounts (most explicit)
.PP
Style 1 just calculates the gain.
Styles 2\-4 calculate the gain and compare it with what you wrote,
potentially catching more errors.
.PP
\f[B]TLDR:\f[R] use Style 1 for brevity and to avoid most problems, or
Style 3 for best error checking while still being concise.
.SS Style 1: No gain postings
.IP
.EX
2026\-02\-01 sell
    assets:stocks   \-1 AAPL {$50} \(at $60
    assets:cash     $60
.EE
.SS Style 2: rgain posting, non\-G account
.IP
.EX
2026\-02\-01 sell
    assets:stocks     \-1 AAPL {$50} \(at $60
    assets:cash      $60
    gains           $\-10
.EE
.PP
Another example, this time with multiple gain postings:
.IP
.EX
2026\-02\-01 sell from two lots
    assets:stocks     \-5 AAPL {$50} \(at $60
    assets:stocks     \-3 AAPL {$55} \(at $60
    assets:cash     $480
    gains           $\-50          ; gain on first lot
    gains           $\-15          ; gain on second lot
.EE
.PP
hledger identifies these \(dqundeclared\(dq gain postings by their
characteristics: postings whose account type is not Asset, Liability, or
Equity (or a subtype); and which have not been classified as lot
postings (acquire, transfer, dispose); and without which the remaining
postings in the entry balance to zero.
.PP
So above, the \f[CR]gains\f[R] postings are detected, their amounts are
checked against the calculated value, and an unrealised gain posting is
added to balance them.
Any other imbalance \- eg a typo in non\-gain postings \(em is reported
as a standard balance error, not silently absorbed as gain.
.PP
This heuristic detection of gain postings can fail (eg when the entry
has an undeclared gain posting and also a fee posting like
\f[CR]expenses:fees\f[R]).
You can check it with \f[CR]hledger print \-a\f[R].
It is just a convenience so that you don\(aqt have to always declare a
type G account, for simple entries.
.SS Style 3: rgain posting, G account
.IP
.EX
account revenues:gain  ; type:G

2026\-02\-01 sell
    assets:stocks     \-1 AAPL {$50} \(at $60
    assets:cash      $60
    revenues:gain   $\-10
.EE
.SS Style 4: rgain and ugain postings, G and U accounts
.IP
.EX
account revenues:gain           ; type:G
account equity:unrealised\-gain  ; type:U

2026\-02\-01 sell
    assets:stocks            \-1 AAPL {$50} \(at $60
    assets:cash              $60
    revenues:gain           $\-10
    equity:unrealised\-gain   $10
.EE
.SS Gain postings and the roi command
When using the roi command with a journal that records lots, make sure
\f[CR]\-\-pnl\f[R] matches both gain accounts, eg:
.IP
.EX
$ hledger roi \-\-inv assets:stocks \-\-pnl \(aqrevenues:gain|equity:unrealised\-gain\(aq
.EE
.PP
Otherwise the unrealised\-gain postings added to each disposal (see
above) are counted as cash flows in and out of the investment,
distorting the report.
.SS Lot postings and balance assertions
On a dispose or transfer posting without an explicit lot subaccount, a
balance assertion always refers to the parent account\(aqs balance.
So if lot subaccounts are added with \f[CR]\-\-lots\f[R], the assertion
is not affected.
.PP
By contrast, in a journal entry where the lot subaccounts are recorded
explicitly, a balance assertion refers to the lot subaccount\(aqs
balance.
.PP
This means that \f[CR]hledger print \-\-lots\f[R], if it adds explicit
lot subaccounts to a journal entry, could potentially change the meaning
of balance assertions, breaking them.
To avoid this, in such cases it will move the balance assertion to a new
zero\-amount posting to the parent account (and make sure it\(aqs
subaccount\-inclusive).
(So eg
\f[CR]hledger \-f\- print \-\-lots \-x | hledger \-f\- check assertions\f[R]
will still pass.)
.SS Lot reporting example
A simple scenario: two acquisitions and a disposal.
.PP
Here is a version using explicit lot subaccounts.
Note this notation can only dispose by specifically identifying the lots
involved:
.IP
.EX
2026\-01\-15 buy low
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}      10 AAPL
    assets:cash                       \-$500

2026\-02\-01 buy high
    assets:stocks:{2026\-02\-01, $60}      10 AAPL
    assets:cash                       \-$600

2026\-03\-01 sell some using specific identification
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}      \-5 AAPL \(at $70
    assets:cash                        $350
.EE
.PP
Here is a version using cost basis annotations:
.IP
.EX
2026\-01\-15 buy low
    assets:stocks      10 AAPL {$50}
    assets:cash     \-$500

2026\-02\-01 buy high
    assets:stocks      10 AAPL {$60}
    assets:cash     \-$600

2026\-03\-01 sell some using default method (FIFO)
    assets:stocks      \-5 AAPL {} \(at $70
    assets:cash      $350
.EE
.PP
And here it is with a lots tag on the commodity:
.IP
.EX
commodity AAPL         ; lots:

2026\-01\-15 buy low
    assets:stocks      10 AAPL
    assets:cash     \-$500

2026\-02\-01 buy high
    assets:stocks      10 AAPL
    assets:cash     \-$600

2026\-03\-01 sell some using AAPL\(aqs method (FIFO)
    assets:stocks      \-5 AAPL \(at $70
    assets:cash      $350
.EE
.PP
In all cases, \f[CR]balance \-\-lots\f[R] shows the current lot
balances:
.IP
.EX
$ hledger bal assets:stocks \-\-lots \-N
              5 AAPL  assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}
             10 AAPL  assets:stocks:{2026\-02\-01, $60}
.EE
.PP
holdings shows a fuller overview of them, with acquisition dates and
cost basis (and, when market prices are recorded, current value and
unrealised gain):
.IP
.EX
$ hledger holdings \-e 2026\-04\-01 \-\-lots
Holdings on 2026\-03\-31

                                 ||       Date  Age    Units  Unit cost  Price  Cost  Value  Weight  UGain  UGain%  RGain  XIRR
=================================++=============================================================================================
 assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50} || 2026\-01\-15  75d   5 AAPL        $50         $250                                 $100
 assets:stocks:{2026\-02\-01, $60} || 2026\-02\-01  58d  10 AAPL        $60         $600
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-++\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
                                 ||                                             $850                                 $100
.EE
.PP
and \f[CR]print \-x \-\-lots\f[R] shows the inferred lot subaccounts and
gain postings.
5 shares were acquired at $50 and sold at $70 = 5 × ($70 \- $50) = $100
gain (remember that revenue amounts appear negative).
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print desc:sell \-x \-\-lots
2026\-03\-01 sell some (FIFO, selects oldest lot first)
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}    \-5 AAPL {2026\-01\-15, $50} \(at $70
    assets:cash                                                   $350
    revenues:gain                                                $\-100
    equity:unrealised\-gain                                        $100
.EE
.PP
Use \f[CR]\-a\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] (short for
\f[CR]\-\-explicit \-\-lots \-\-verbose\-tags\f[R]) to also see the lot
posting classifications (ptype tags), useful for troubleshooting:
.IP
.EX
$ hledger print desc:sell \-a
2026\-03\-01 sell some (FIFO, selects oldest lot first)
    assets:stocks:{2026\-01\-15, $50}    \-5 AAPL {2026\-01\-15, $50} \(at $70  ; ptype: dispose
    assets:cash                                                   $350
    revenues:gain                                                $\-100  ; ptype: rgain, generated\-posting:
    equity:unrealised\-gain                                        $100  ; ptype: ugain, generated\-posting:
.EE
.SH PART 4: COMMANDS
.PP
Here are hledger\(aqs standard subcommands.
You can list these by running \f[CR]hledger help commands\f[R].
If you have installed more add\-on commands, they also will be listed.
.PP
In the following command docs, each command\(aqs specific options are
shown.
Most commands also support the general options described above, though
some of them might have no effect.
(Usually if there\(aqs a sensible way for a general option to affect a
command, it will.)
You can list all of a command\(aqs options by running
\f[CR]hledger CMD \-h\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Help commands\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
help (h) \- show documentation
.PP
\f[B]User interface commands\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
repl \- run multiple commands from an interactive prompt
.IP \(bu 2
run \- run multiple commands from a file or command line
.IP \(bu 2
